ts to  rebuild the bridge quickly on no notice  plus the cost in lives plus the the loss  commuting times the lost business all of  that and it's a problem that's common to  infrastructure you I think there's  something like 80,000 bridges that need  to be fixed and it becomes this this  long term versus short term cost  calculation saying hey I'm sorry we  don't have any money in the budget right  now to deal with this we've got to deal  with this other much more urgent thing  and then of course when something falls  apart because you haven't dealt with it  early on then that only creates more of  a vicious spiral and so I think we need  to think somewhat differently about how  we account for things in policymaking to  look harder at avoided costs the other  question is that when there's a crisis  there some how often are these magical  pools of money from you know federal  disaster funds and blah blah blah what  if we were to instead you know shrink  the federal disaster fund pool and  reallocate some of that towards towards  prevention and and with with aid in  other countries as well Steven Covey has  this this really great tool out of the  seven Habits of Highly Effective leaders  which he he has this little grid you  know is this urgent and this is this  important and he points out how much  time we spend in the urgent category  whether or not it's important and so  there are some things I think that you  do need to just let go so you know what  this is this is it you know in business  sometimes that's called creative  destruction and it can be a very good  thing you you want to cushion the people  who are affected by it as much as you  can  but find ways to allocate more time more  budget more energy more priority  whatever to the things that are  important but not necessarily immediate  and I think we could do a lot better at  every level from from policymaking at  the global to the national to the  regional and local level to businesses  to you know to personal life frankly I  mean one of the great minds that comes  up all the time is is retirement  planning or you know saving for college  you know there are all sorts of things  that are very relevant on a personal  level and you know obviously those  aren't the big giant things that affect  everyone but but for an individual who's  affected it's it's pretty darn important  so I think you you can be flexible in  your definition there but if someone who  invests in the areas that you're talking  about one of the ways I've had to  reconcile this for many years is to  argue that you don't go off and just try  to invent whole new technologies  immediately that leads to Solyndra  problems and all kinds of crazy things  especially when politicians do it you do  the things that are cost effective from  a capital allotment set we know right  away energy efficiency pricing all these  things you buy time you don't turn off  the nuclear power plants today because  they've got twenty more years of to run  and in the mean that's 20% of the  electricity in the country even if you  don't like nuclear power you don't you  know Al Gore I'm sorry you can't do that  but if you do by 20 or 40 more years  what matters to you - for climate change  is where we are technologically in 2050  not not somehow doing it immediately  today and by the way our renewable  energy program I had the the  transmission guys from from Germany come  in ten years ago now saying we've put  twenty five thousand megawatts of wind  North Sea wind in place we spent more  money than you can imagine and now and  we can't even say it's greenhouse gas  positive because we have so much standby  generation diesel-powered that we have  to keep in Germany to meet peak load  when the wind isn't blowing so so  policymakers often  don't weren't gray rhinos solvers in the  way that Michelle's talking about you  know parse it look at the long term  you've got 20 30 40 years for your  technology side so put in a good program  l